INT. GONDOLA CABLE CAR - ABOVE A GORGE - DAY

Stalled mid-span. Wind rocks the cabin. A hundred meters of nothing below.

TESS, 30s, climbing jacket, sits on the floor with her back to the door. RUBEN, 40s, her husband, stands with his palms on the glass. AMARA, 16, headphones around her neck, picks at a hole in her sleeve. WEN, 70s, in a wool cap, eats sunflower seeds with great deliberation.

A fifth figure sits apart: a STRANGER, 50s, in a rain shell, sunglasses on, a duffel on his lap. He hasn't said a word in two hours.

RUBEN
The next car's at the station. They said an hour.

TESS
They said an hour at the last hour.

Wen spits a shell into his palm. Amara watches the bag in the stranger's lap.

AMARA
What's in the bag?

The Stranger turns his head. Smiles.

STRANGER
Rope.

TESS
Real rope?

STRANGER
Forty meters. Rated.

RUBEN
Why didn't you say?

STRANGER
You'd have wanted to use it.

The cabin sways. Tess swallows. Nobody speaks. The Stranger takes off his glasses. His eyes are red, tired.

STRANGER (CONT'D)
Delphine. My name. I was the one who rode the line crew when they rebuilt this span in '09.

WEN
You're the engineer.

DELPHINE
I was. I signed the inspection. I come up here every year on the same day.

RUBEN
Why?

DELPHINE
Because the weld under your feet has a crack I wrote off as cosmetic.

Silence. Tess's eyes go slowly to the floor under her. Amara pulls her knees up. Ruben stops breathing.

TESS
(very quiet)
How long?

DELPHINE
It hasn't moved in fifteen years. But then it hasn't been stopped in the wind before, either.

WEN
Is the rope for us?

DELPHINE
It's for one at a time. Down the cable, to the pylon. It's two hundred meters, and a harness I made out of seatbelt.

AMARA
I'll go.

TESS
No.

AMARA
I'm lightest.

RUBEN
Amara, sit.

AMARA
I'm the one who said it, so I'm the one who goes.

DELPHINE
Nobody goes yet. I don't want to be right today. I want to be wrong, and I want a rescue car to come over that hill with a man in a yellow helmet who tells me I'm an idiot.

Out of the gray, a distant engine. Everyone turns.

Wen slowly puts a seed in his mouth.

WEN
I'd like that too.

The engine grows louder. Delphine does not look up. She is watching the floor.
